// Build settings for {{package}}. Scala CLI reads every `//> using` line in this file, and
// scripts/check rejects `//> using` lines anywhere else. scripts/check also keeps its own copy of
// the compiler options below and fails when the two differ, so a removed check cannot pass.
// Agents: change these lines only with the owner's approval.

// Scala 3.9 is the long-term-support line. Keep to exact versions: scripts/check rejects others.
//> using scala 3.9.0
// The JDK comes from mise.toml, so Scala CLI, Metals and CI all use the same pinned one.
//> using jvm system

// Warnings are errors. -Wall turns on the -W checks: unused imports, private and local code,
// discarded values, non-Unit statements, toString in interpolation and more. It does not check
// initialisation order or catch-all handlers.
// -deprecation and -feature give each such warning its location. strictEquality rejects ==
// between types that can never be equal. -source:future rejects syntax that Scala is dropping,
// such as `import x._` and `implicit val`.
//> using options -Werror -Wall -deprecation -feature -language:strictEquality -source:future

// WartRemover bans, checked with types: mutable state (var and mutable collections), null,
// return, while, casts, throw, and calls that throw on ordinary input (Option.get, List.head,
// Try.get, xs(i), xs.updated(i, x), ???). Also Array.toString, "text" + value, and String methods
// that use the machine's default locale or charset. The plugin is built for each exact Scala version: bump it with `using scala`.
//> using plugin org.wartremover:::wartremover:3.6.3
//> using options -P:wartremover:traverser:org.wartremover.warts.Var
//> using options -P:wartremover:traverser:org.wartremover.warts.MutableDataStructures
//> using options -P:wartremover:traverser:org.wartremover.warts.Null
//> using options -P:wartremover:traverser:org.wartremover.warts.Return
//> using options -P:wartremover:traverser:org.wartremover.warts.While
//> using options -P:wartremover:traverser:org.wartremover.warts.AsInstanceOf
//> using options -P:wartremover:traverser:org.wartremover.warts.IsInstanceOf
//> using options -P:wartremover:traverser:org.wartremover.warts.OptionPartial
//> using options -P:wartremover:traverser:org.wartremover.warts.TryPartial
//> using options -P:wartremover:traverser:org.wartremover.warts.EitherProjectionPartial
//> using options -P:wartremover:traverser:org.wartremover.warts.IterableOps
//> using options -P:wartremover:traverser:org.wartremover.warts.TripleQuestionMark
//> using options -P:wartremover:traverser:org.wartremover.warts.ArrayToString
//> using options -P:wartremover:traverser:org.wartremover.warts.StringPlusAny
//> using options -P:wartremover:traverser:org.wartremover.warts.PlatformDefault
//> using options -P:wartremover:traverser:org.wartremover.warts.Throw
//> using options -P:wartremover:traverser:org.wartremover.warts.SeqApply
//> using options -P:wartremover:traverser:org.wartremover.warts.SeqUpdated

// Scala 3 lazy vals still use sun.misc.Unsafe, and JDK 25 prints a warning when a program first
// touches one. The warning is about the Scala library, not this code.
//> using javaOpt --sun-misc-unsafe-memory-access=allow

